      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Chromatic aberration: simulation + correction round-trip\n\nChromatic aberration (CA) is lateral colour fringing caused by a lens refracting different wavelengths at slightly different angles -- red, green, and blue focus at slightly different lateral positions on the sensor.  A calibration file (YAML) encodes per-channel polynomial displacement fields in a format OpenCV 5.0 can read.\n\nPIPELINE: CreateCACalibration writes a degree-1 calibration YAML (+15 red, -10 blue) matching the image resolution (1500x1050) to input/calib/.  Then Load image -> ApplyChromaticAberration (simulates CA by shifting R and B via polynomial displacement maps) -> ChromaticAberrationCorrection (reads the same calibration file from the dropdown, resizes to calibration dimensions, calls cv2.correctChromaticAberration, resizes back) -> AbsDiff against the original to show the round-trip residual (edge artefacts from interpolation).\n\nWhen calibration size matches the image, both Apply and Correct operate at native resolution.  When they differ, both nodes internally resize to calibration size, apply the polynomial, and resize back (a warning is logged).  For best results, create calibration at your image's native resolution.\n\nIMPORTANT: CreateCACalibration must execute FIRST (it is a terminal node with no output). Run it, then run the rest."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
